Beryllium: renderers can enter Capsicum capability mode (opt-in, Phase 2)

Purpose: the first step of the renderer sandbox (plan/14-RENDERER-SANDBOX.md
  increment 2; feature matrix S-REN-01, S-REN-02, S-HRD-03). On FreeBSD
  every renderer already reaches SandboxLinux::InitializeSandbox() through
  RendererMainPlatformDelegate::EnableSandbox(), after PreSandboxInit() and
  before page content and Mojo set-up; the imported FreeBSD code there only
  warms up a few values and lowers RLIMIT_DATA. This patch adds the
  lockdown at that point, for renderers only:
  - warm-up of what the Capsicum survey (freebsd/tests/browser, run
    capsicum --diagnose, owner's machine, 4 November 2026) showed renderers
    read that capability mode refuses: the memory and shared-memory sysctls
    (vm.stats.vm.v_page_count and v_page_size, about 19 reads per renderer;
    kern.ipc.shmmax), uname() (kern.version), the executable path
    (kern.proc.pathname), time zone data (/etc/localtime);
  - base/system on FreeBSD keeps those answers once read: total physical
    memory, kern.ipc.shmmax and uname() never change while the browser
    runs, and their failure paths are NOTREACHED() or CHECK, which would
    end a confined renderer. Available memory, which does change, reports
    0 in capability mode instead of ending the process;
  - a utrace(2) marker ("BERYLLIUM-LOCKDOWN") at the lockdown point, always,
    so the survey can split each renderer's trace into before and after
    the lockdown; utrace is a no-op unless the process is traced;
  - cap_enter(), checked with cap_getmode(), only when the feature
    BerylliumCapsicumRenderer is enabled
    (--enable-features=BerylliumCapsicumRenderer). Daily use is unchanged
    until the lockdown is shown to work. When it is requested and fails,
    the renderer ends (fail closed); when it succeeds, one log line
    "beryllium-sandbox: renderer pid=N capability_mode=1";
  - with BerylliumCapsicumTrap also enabled, PROC_TRAPCAP_CTL, so the first
    call capability mode refuses ends the renderer with SIGTRAP and a
    backtrace instead of failing quietly (for finding the next warm-up);
  - from Alex S's 2026 Capsicum prototype for the FreeBSD port
    (plan/15-CAPSICUM-PROTOTYPE-EVALUATION.md): caph_cache_tzdata() and
    caph_cache_catpages() in the warm-up, dlopen of /lib/libgcc_s.so.1
    (libthr loads it by path for unwinding when a thread exits), and
    caph_limit_stdio() before cap_enter() (a failure there is logged, not
    fatal).
  - before cap_enter(), every directory descriptor is closed and counted
    (from kern.proc.filedesc): capability mode still allows lookups
    beneath a directory descriptor, and the survey of 0.20.3.3 found
    renderers holding /, /usr, /usr/local and /usr/local/etc, inherited
    from the browser (beryllium-close-inherited-descriptors.patch stops
    the inheritance; this is the check behind it). The log line reports
    closed_directories=N; a table that cannot be read fails the lockdown.
  Later increments make it the default and fail closed in the production
  package (charter D5), add the descriptor allowlist, launcher hygiene,
  kernel attestation and status reporting.
Stage: 4 (Beryllium series). Depends on the imported FreeBSD patches
  patch-sandbox_policy_freebsd_sandbox__freebsd.cc,
  patch-base_system_sys__info__freebsd.cc and
  patch-base_system_sys__info__posix.cc (stage 2).
Refreshed against: Chromium 156.0.8078.79, Helium 0.20.3 (2f81b9ca),
  FreeBSD ports 6ad3e0c8, using freebsd/tools/patch-workbench. Not yet
  compiled: the next Poudriere build is the first.
Test: freebsd/tests/browser/run capsicum --diagnose (the survey splits at
  the marker); with --browser-arg=--enable-features=BerylliumCapsicumRenderer,
  test_50_sandbox shows renderers in capability mode and the rest of the
  suite shows what still breaks. Owner's machine, 0.20.3.3, 4 November
  2026: with the feature, every renderer in capability mode and the whole
  suite passing; the survey's after-lockdown list holds only
  cpuset_getaffinity (18) and one open.
security-review: required (sandbox setup, process confinement)

